---

# 06. Repository Structure Is Not Decoration

Directories must represent meaningful ownership boundaries.

Do not create folders merely because:

```text
"it looks organized"
```

Every major directory should answer:

> What responsibility belongs here?

---

# 07. Ownership Principle

Every important artifact must have an identifiable owner.

Examples:

```text
domain/
→ Domain architecture

components/
→ UI implementation

services/
→ Application/service behavior

data/
→ Data persistence and contracts

tests/
→ Verification

docs/
→ System knowledge
```

---

# 08. Dependency Direction

Dependencies should generally flow inward toward stable domain concepts.

Conceptually:

```text
UI
 ↓
Application
 ↓
Domain
 ↓
Infrastructure
```

Infrastructure should not dictate domain behavior.

UI should not define business rules.

---

# 09. Domain Independence

The domain model must remain as independent as reasonably practical from:

```text
React
Next.js
database vendor
AI provider
UI component library
deployment provider
```

The domain should represent AEGIS concepts, not framework-specific implementation details.

---

# 10. UI Boundary

UI components are responsible for:

```text
presentation
interaction
visual state
accessibility
user feedback
navigation
```

UI components must not independently invent:

```text
business rules
threat classification logic
clinical rules
security policy
database queries
AI decision logic
```

---

# 11. Component Responsibility

A component should have one primary responsibility.

Avoid components that simultaneously perform:

```text
data fetching
domain calculations
AI calls
database mutations
navigation
visual rendering
audit logging
```

unless there is a clear architectural reason.

---

# 12. Domain Logic Rule

Critical domain behavior belongs in domain/application services rather than scattered throughout UI components.

Bad:

```text
Button
 ↓
complex threat classification
```

Preferred:

```text
Button
 ↓
application action
 ↓
domain service
 ↓
validated result
 ↓
UI update
```

---

# 13. Screen Rule

A screen is a composition of capabilities.

A screen must not become a dumping ground for:

```text
API logic
database logic
AI prompts
domain rules
authentication rules
```

---

# 14. 13-Screen Integrity Rule

The approved AEGIS 13-screen registry is authoritative.

No AI agent may:

```text
rename screens
merge screens
split screens
remove screens
add screens
change screen responsibilities
```

without an explicit approved change.

---

# 15. Screen Naming

Screen names must match the approved registry.

Do not create alternative terminology merely for convenience.

The following should not happen:

```text
Global Monitoring
Global Monitor
Monitoring Dashboard
Command Dashboard
Global Threat View
```

if they all refer to the same approved screen.

---

# 16. Route Integrity

Routes must map predictably to approved screen identities.

Do not create hidden duplicate routes for the same screen unless explicitly required.

---

# 17. State Ownership

Every state variable must have a clear owner.

State should be classified as:

```text
Local UI State
Application State
Domain State
Server State
Session State
Persistent State
```

Do not place all state into a single global store merely because it is convenient.

---

# 18. Global State Rule

Global state must only contain information that genuinely needs global accessibility.

Do not put:

```text
temporary modal state
hover state
input text
button loading state
local animation state
```

into global state without justification.

---

# 19. Domain State Rule

Domain state must follow Document 02 and Document 05.

Agents must not create new state transitions simply because a UI interaction needs one.

---

# 20. State Transition Rule

Critical state transitions must be explicit.

Conceptually:

```text
CURRENT STATE
      +
VALID EVENT
      ↓
VALID TRANSITION
      ↓
NEW STATE
      ↓
SIDE EFFECTS
      ↓
AUDIT / OBSERVABILITY
```

Invalid transitions must be rejected or handled according to the approved specification.

---

# 21. Data Ownership

Data must have a clear source of truth.

Do not maintain conflicting copies of important domain data.

Example:

```text
Server
   ↓
authoritative threat state

UI
   ↓
presentation of threat state
```

The UI should not silently invent a different threat state.

---

# 22. Mock Data Rule

Mock data is permitted during development.

But mock data must be clearly identifiable.

Recommended:

```text
data/fixtures/
```

Never hide production-like fake data inside application logic.

---

# 23. Mock Data Must Match Contracts

Mock data must conform to the same schemas as real data.

This is mandatory.

The following is prohibited:

```text
mock shape ≠ production shape
```

because it creates false confidence.

---

# 24. Hardcoded Data Rule

Hardcoded domain data inside UI components is prohibited when the data should originate from:

```text
API
domain state
configuration
database
service
```

---

# 25. API Rule

Frontend code must consume approved API/service contracts.

Do not invent endpoints because a component needs data.

---

# 26. API Client Rule

API access should be centralized through appropriate service/client abstractions.

Avoid scattered:

```text
fetch(...)
fetch(...)
fetch(...)
```

throughout unrelated UI components.

---

# 27. Error Handling

Every network operation must account for:

```text
loading
success
empty
failure
timeout
unauthorized
forbidden
validation failure
unexpected response
```

where applicable.

---

# 28. No Silent Failures

Errors must not disappear silently.

At minimum, the system should:

```text
detect
record
surface appropriately
recover or fail safely
```

according to the relevant architecture.

---

# 29. Security Boundary

Security-sensitive logic must not live exclusively in the client.

Never rely on:

```text
hidden UI elements
disabled buttons
frontend route guards
client-side role checks
```

as the sole security mechanism.

---

# 30. Authorization Rule

Authorization must be enforced at the appropriate backend/service boundary.

UI authorization exists for user experience.

Backend authorization exists for security.

Both should be consistent.

---

# 31. Authentication Rule

Authentication behavior must follow Document 10.

AI agents may not replace authentication mechanisms with improvised implementations.

---

# 32. Secrets Rule

Never commit:

```text
API keys
tokens
passwords
private credentials
certificates
private keys
production secrets
```

to the repository.

---

# 33. Environment Variables

Environment-specific configuration should use appropriate environment configuration.

Never hardcode environment-specific secrets.

---

# 34. AI Provider Rule

AI provider credentials must never appear in:

```text
frontend bundles
client-side code
UI configuration
Git history
screenshots
prompts
```

---

# 35. AI Integration Boundary

AI must be accessed through an explicit application boundary.

Conceptually:

```text
AEGIS Application
      ↓
Intelligence Service
      ↓
AI Provider Adapter
      ↓
Model
```

Do not scatter provider-specific calls across components.

---

# 36. AI Provider Independence

Business logic should not depend directly on a specific model provider.

The application should be able to replace or disable an AI provider without rewriting the entire domain.

---

# 37. AI Output Validation

Never trust AI output merely because it is syntactically valid text.

Where AI output feeds application logic:

```text
AI output
 ↓
schema validation
 ↓
semantic validation
 ↓
policy validation
 ↓
application
```

---

# 38. AI Hallucination Rule

AI agents must not fabricate:

```text
API endpoints
database fields
domain entities
clinical recommendations
security policies
requirements
screen behavior
architecture decisions
```

If something is unknown:

```text
STOP
REPORT
ASK
```

---

# 39. AI Agent Operating Modes

Agents operate in one of three modes:

### READ

Agent may inspect but not modify.

### PLAN

Agent may analyze and propose changes but not implement.

### WRITE

Agent may modify approved files within defined scope.

---

# 40. Default AI Permission

The default mode should be:

```text
READ
```

Agents should receive write authority only when a task explicitly requires it.

---

# 41. Scope Rule

Every implementation task should define:

```text
IN SCOPE
OUT OF SCOPE
```

An agent must not modify unrelated areas.

---

# 42. File Ownership

If a task concerns:

```text
components/threat/
```

the agent should not casually rewrite:

```text
services/auth/
infrastructure/
database/
```

unless the dependency requires it and the change is explicitly reported.

---

# 43. Unrelated Change Rule

If an agent discovers an unrelated issue:

```text
record it
```

Do not automatically fix it during the current task.

This prevents scope explosion.

---

# 44. Dependency Rule

Adding a dependency requires justification.

Before adding:

```text
npm package
library
framework
service
SDK
```

the agent must determine:

```text
Why is it needed?
Does AEGIS already have an equivalent?
What is the maintenance cost?
What security implications exist?
Does it affect bundle size?
Does it conflict with architecture?
```

---

# 45. Dependency Minimization

AEGIS should prefer:

```text
existing dependency
```

over:

```text
new dependency
```

when both provide an adequate solution.

---

# 46. No Dependency Duplication

Do not install multiple libraries for the same fundamental purpose without explicit justification.

Examples:

```text
multiple date libraries
multiple state libraries
multiple animation libraries
multiple HTTP clients
multiple validation libraries
```

---

# 47. Design System Rule

All production UI must use the approved AEGIS design system.

Agents must not independently invent:

```text
colors
spacing
typography
border radii
shadows
buttons
cards
status indicators
```

when an approved token/component exists.

---

# 48. Visual Direction

AEGIS visual identity is:

```text
Minimalist
HUD-inspired
High-density telemetry
Clinical authority
Deep Obsidian
Surgical White
Antigen Amber for alerts
```

The following are prohibited unless explicitly approved:

```text
gaming aesthetics
cyberpunk decoration
unnecessary neon
excessive gradients
consumer-dashboard styling
visual noise
decorative 3D that reduces clarity
```

---

# 49. Accessibility

Accessibility is a system requirement.

Production interfaces must consider:

```text
keyboard navigation
focus management
semantic HTML
screen-reader compatibility
contrast
motion preferences
error communication
touch targets
```

---

# 50. Responsive Rule

Responsive behavior must follow the approved application architecture.

Do not simply shrink desktop layouts until they technically fit.

The information hierarchy must remain usable.

---

# 51. Animation Rule

Motion must communicate:

```text
state
transition
priority
feedback
system activity
```

Motion must not exist merely because a library makes animation easy.

---

# 52. Telemetry Rule

Operationally important events must follow Document 12.

Do not create arbitrary analytics events with inconsistent naming.

---

# 53. Audit Rule

Security-sensitive and operationally important actions must follow Document 10.

Agents must not introduce hidden actions that bypass the audit model.

---

# 54. Logging Rule

Logs must:

```text
be structured
contain useful context
avoid secrets
avoid unnecessary sensitive information
support investigation
```

---

# 55. Error Logging

Errors should preserve enough context to diagnose failures without exposing unnecessary sensitive information.

---

# 56. Testing Constitution

No significant feature is considered complete until its required tests exist and pass.

Testing must cover appropriate levels:

```text
unit
integration
contract
end-to-end
accessibility
security
AI evaluation
```

as applicable.

---

# 57. Test Placement

Tests should live in predictable locations.

Recommended:

```text
tests/
├── unit/
├── integration/
├── e2e/
├── accessibility/
├── security/
└── intelligence/
```

---

# 58. Test Naming

Tests must describe behavior rather than implementation details.

Prefer:

```text
should classify an anomaly as requiring investigation
```

over:

```text
should call function xyz()
```

when behavior is the actual requirement.

---

# 59. Test Isolation

Tests should not depend unnecessarily on:

```text
production
external AI provider
live healthcare data
uncontrolled external services
```

Use mocks, fixtures, adapters, or controlled environments where appropriate.

---

# 60. End-to-End Requirement

The core AEGIS operational journey must eventually have end-to-end coverage:

```text
Detection
 ↓
Investigation
 ↓
Threat Assessment
 ↓
Classification
 ↓
Recommendation
 ↓
Human Decision
 ↓
Response
 ↓
Monitoring
 ↓
Recovery
 ↓
Learning
```

---

# 61. AI Testing

AI behavior requires more than ordinary unit testing.

Tests should include:

```text
expected output
invalid output
missing context
ambiguous evidence
contradictory evidence
provider failure
timeout
low-confidence scenario
adversarial input
```

---

# 62. Build Rule

The repository must build successfully before a change is accepted.

A change that:

```text
looks correct
```

but:

```text
breaks the build
```

is not complete.

---

# 63. Type Safety

Where the selected stack supports strong typing, domain and service contracts should be strongly typed.

Avoid unnecessary:

```text
any
unknown
unvalidated casts
```

especially around critical domain data.

---

# 64. Validation Boundary

External data must be validated before entering trusted application logic.

Conceptually:

```text
External Input
      ↓
Validation
      ↓
Normalization
      ↓
Trusted Domain
```

---

# 65. Input Validation

Validate:

```text
API input
forms
URL parameters
query parameters
external service responses
AI outputs
uploaded data
```

where applicable.

---

# 66. No Trust Boundary Leakage

Do not allow raw external data to flow directly into:

```text
critical domain decisions
database writes
security-sensitive operations
AI prompts
```

without appropriate validation and handling.

---

# 67. Database Rule

Database access must follow the approved data architecture.

Do not let UI components query the database directly.

---

# 68. Migration Rule

Database schema changes must be explicit and versioned.

Never manually change production structure without a controlled migration process.

---

# 69. Data Integrity

Important domain records should preserve appropriate:

```text
identity
timestamps
relationships
status
history
audit information
```

according to Document 06.

---

# 70. Time Rule

Time-dependent domain events should use consistent time representation.

Avoid mixing:

```text
local browser time
server time
database time
```

without explicit normalization.

---

# 71. ID Rule

Identifiers must be generated and managed according to the data architecture.

Do not create ad-hoc IDs in UI code for persistent entities.

---

# 72. Event Rule

Domain events should represent meaningful system events.

Do not create events merely because a button was clicked unless the click itself represents meaningful domain behavior.

---

# 73. Side Effect Rule

Critical side effects must be explicit.

Examples:

```text
notification
audit event
external API call
state mutation
response action
```

must not be hidden inside unrelated presentation code.

---

# 74. Transaction Rule

Where multiple operations must succeed together, use the appropriate transactional or consistency strategy defined by the architecture.

Do not assume sequential frontend requests are equivalent to a transaction.

---

# 75. Caching Rule

Caching must not create stale or contradictory critical state.

Cache strategy must respect the distinction between:

```text
authoritative server state
temporary UI state
derived state
```

---

# 76. Offline / Failure Behavior

Where applicable, the application must define what happens when:

```text
network fails
API fails
AI unavailable
database unavailable
telemetry unavailable
```

Agents must not invent fallback behavior.

---

# 77. Graceful Degradation

When a non-critical subsystem fails, AEGIS should degrade according to approved architecture rather than collapsing unnecessarily.

However:

> Safety-critical uncertainty must never be hidden behind a fake successful state.

---

# 78. No Fake Success

Never show:

```text
Action successful
Threat resolved
Response executed
Analysis complete
```

unless the underlying operation actually succeeded according to its contract.

---

# 79. Simulation Rule

If an operation is simulated:

```text
SIMULATED
```

must remain distinguishable from:

```text
EXECUTED
```

This is especially important during MVP development.

---

# 80. Clinical / Operational Boundary

AEGIS is decision support.

The repository must preserve the distinction between:

```text
system observation
system analysis
AI recommendation
human decision
actual operational execution
```

Do not collapse them into one state.

---

# 81. No Autonomous High-Stakes Action

Unless explicitly authorized and architecturally defined, AI must not independently execute consequential real-world healthcare or biosecurity actions.

The system should support human-controlled workflows.

---

# 82. Auditability

Important actions must be traceable.

A reviewer should eventually be able to determine:

```text
what happened
when
who initiated it
what data informed it
what recommendation existed
what decision was made
what action occurred
what result followed
```

where required by the architecture.

---

# 83. Reproducibility

Important system behavior should be reproducible enough for debugging and investigation.

Record appropriate:

```text
version
configuration
model
prompt version
input reference
output
timestamp
```

where applicable and safe.

---

# 84. AI Prompt Repository

Important production AI prompts belong in:

```text
prompts/
```

and should be version-controlled.

---

# 85. Prompt Naming

Use predictable names:

```text
threat-assessment.v1.md
anomaly-analysis.v1.md
incident-summary.v1.md
response-support.v1.md
feedback-analysis.v1.md
```

---

# 86. Prompt Changes

Never silently edit a production-critical prompt.

A meaningful change should result in:

```text
new version
evaluation
review
approval
```

when appropriate.

---

# 87. Agent Rules File

The repository should contain a machine-readable or machine-readable-friendly agent instruction file.

Examples depending on the selected development environment may include:

```text
AGENTS.md
```

or equivalent agent configuration supported by the chosen toolchain.

The file should point agents toward:

```text
architecture
repository structure
coding standards
testing requirements
security rules
AI boundaries
```

---

# 88. AGENTS.md Principle

The agent instruction file should be concise.

It should not duplicate every architecture document.

Instead:

```text
AGENTS.md
 ↓
rules
 ↓
architecture references
```

---

# 89. Agent Startup Procedure

Every AI coding agent should begin with:

```text
1. Read repository instructions.
2. Read project state.
3. Identify relevant architecture documents.
4. Inspect repository.
5. Confirm task scope.
6. Plan.
7. Implement.
8. Test.
9. Review diff.
10. Report.
```

---

# 90. Agent Shutdown Procedure

Before finishing:

```text
1. Run relevant tests.
2. Run build/type checks.
3. Inspect changed files.
4. Confirm no unrelated changes.
5. Update documentation if necessary.
6. Update project state.
7. Report unresolved issues.
```

---

# 91. Agent Report Format

Every significant implementation task should end with:

```text
IMPLEMENTED

Summary:
...

Files changed:
...

Architecture affected:
...

Tests:
...

Validation:
...

Known issues:
...

Documentation updated:
...

Recommended next task:
...
```

---

# 92. Forbidden Agent Behaviors

AI agents must never:

```text
invent requirements
invent APIs
invent domain entities
invent security policies
invent clinical protocols
delete architecture documents
rewrite unrelated subsystems
remove tests merely to make builds pass
disable security checks
hide errors
commit secrets
silently add dependencies
silently change global state architecture
silently rename approved concepts
```

---

# 93. Forbidden Shortcut

This is prohibited:

```text
test fails
 ↓
delete test
 ↓
build passes
```

Correct:

```text
test fails
 ↓
understand failure
 ↓
fix implementation
 ↓
run test
```

---

# 94. Forbidden Shortcut

This is prohibited:

```text
TypeScript error
 ↓
cast everything to any
```

Instead:

```text
understand type mismatch
 ↓
fix contract
```

---

# 95. Forbidden Shortcut

This is prohibited:

```text
API missing
 ↓
hardcode fake production behavior
```

Instead:

```text
identify contract
 ↓
implement approved mock/adapter
```

---

# 96. Forbidden Shortcut

This is prohibited:

```text
UI requires data
 ↓
invent new database structure
```

Instead:

```text
inspect data architecture
 ↓
identify existing contract
 ↓
escalate if missing
```

---

# 97. Forbidden Shortcut

This is prohibited:

```text
AI output looks convincing
 ↓
trust it
```

Instead:

```text
validate
 ↓
evaluate
 ↓
apply policy
```

---

# 98. Code Review Rule

Code review should evaluate:

```text
correctness
architecture
security
state
data
performance
accessibility
testing
maintainability
observability
```

not merely:

```text
does it run?
```

---

# 99. Pull Request Rule

A meaningful pull request should describe:

```text
What changed?
Why?
Which architecture documents are affected?
Which requirements are satisfied?
What tests were added?
What remains?
```

---

# 100. Commit Rule

Commits should represent coherent changes.

Avoid:

```text
fix stuff
update
changes
AI work
final final
```

Prefer:

```text
feat: implement threat classification state transition
fix: handle failed anomaly ingestion
test: add response workflow integration coverage
refactor: isolate intelligence provider adapter
```

---

# 101. Commit Size

Prefer small, understandable commits.

Large AI-generated commits are harder to:

```text
review
debug
revert
understand
```

---

# 102. Branch Rule

Feature work should be isolated from stable branches according to the project's Git workflow.

Do not experiment directly on the stable release branch.

---

# 103. Rollback Principle

Every meaningful deployment must have a rollback strategy appropriate to the deployment architecture.

---

# 104. Documentation Synchronization

Code and architecture must not permanently diverge.

If code changes a documented behavior:

```text
update documentation
```

If documentation changes:

```text
identify implementation impact
```

---

# 105. Architecture Drift

Architecture drift occurs when:

```text
code behavior
≠
approved architecture
```

without an explicit decision.

Architecture drift must be treated as technical debt or a defect.

---

# 106. Drift Detection

At milestones compare:

```text
Documents
vs
Repository
vs
Tests
vs
Running application
```

All four should describe substantially the same system.

---

# 107. Definition of Done

A feature is not done because:

```text
the screen looks good
```

or:

```text
the code compiles
```

A feature is done when:

```text
requirement satisfied
+
architecture aligned
+
state correct
+
data correct
+
API correct
+
UI correct
+
security satisfied
+
tests passing
+
observability appropriate
+
documentation synchronized
```

---

